{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310857,"nonce":"epwxeXlJpimQR4prW+ovOEnly9c=","title":"Per-function LLVM promotion landed in 00c0bb48; it is correct and avoids a whole-module crash, but wins no speed until inner functions are ...","message":"**LANDED 00c0bb48.** `process_llvm_queue` compiles the queued functions, their\nOSR resume points, and any callee nothing else can serve — instead of compiling\nevery function in the program. `RAYZOR_LLVM_WHOLE_MODULE=1` puts it back on the\nold path for comparison.\n\n**Per-function is the safer path**, which was the surprise: it runs deltablue\nclean, while whole-module promotion **segfaults deltablue** (signal 11 — the\nfault reported at the Maximum tier earlier, now reproducible on demand with\nthat env var). That crash is still OPEN.\n\nClean numbers, quiet NUC, `blazing_threshold: 10`:\n\n| bench | tiered (per-fn) | tiered (whole-module) | cranelift | llvm |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fibonacci | 255.64 | — | 254.57 | 268.31 |\n| nbody | 414.79 | 386.51 | 397.90 | **349.63** |\n| deltablue | 17.93 | **signal 11** | 17.11 | 16.61 |\n| mandelbrot | 252.62 | — | 252.20 | **218.35** |\n\n**It wins no speed, and the reason is structural, not a bug.** `record_call`\nfires only from `execute_function`, so the only function that ever goes hot is\nthe ENTRY. The queue can therefore only ever contain `main`. Per-function\npromotion compiles `main`, and because Cranelift emits **direct** calls, main's\ncallees stay on Cranelift — on nbody all the FP work is in `advance()`, which\nnever moves. Whole-module reaches 386 precisely by moving everything, which is\nalso what makes it crash.\n\n**THE NEXT LEVER: profile inner functions.** Nothing else unlocks nbody's 349\nor mandelbrot's 218. A workable design without a callback into the runtime:\nincrement a baked-in per-function counter at entry (the name-keyed leaked-slot\nmachinery in `ir/osr.rs`, `helper_slot_addr(name, key)`, already does this), and\nhave the existing drain — which runs on the main thread at a safe point — scan\nthe counters and queue what crossed the threshold. **Weigh the cost first:**\ncommit eebd36be won 25% on fib by removing ONE push per call, so an increment\nper call is not free, and on fib our LLVM is slower than our Cranelift anyway.\nInstrument the Cranelift tiers only, so the promoted code carries no counter.\n\n**LATENT, NOT LIVE — and it is a PREREQUISITE of the profiling work above, not\na separate bug.** The only audit finding that survived adversarial refutation:\nLLVM lowers every `static var` to a module-private zero-initialised `Internal`\nglobal, so one LLVM module per drain would split statics — a function promoted\nin drain 1 and one promoted in drain 3 stop sharing static state with each other\nand with still-Cranelift code.\n\nIt cannot be triggered today. `record_call` queues only when `should_promote`\nreturns a tier HIGHER than the current one, and only the entry is ever counted,\nso `main` crosses to Maximum once, is queued once, and never again: **exactly\none drain per process, one LLVM module.** Verified in the tier-event log — a\nsingle `llvm_queue_start`, no second. Do not try to write a failing test for\nthis before inner-function profiling exists; there isn't one.\n\nWhen it does activate, the fix is forward declaration: define the global once,\nand in every later module declare it `External` with no initializer and\n`add_global_mapping` it to that one storage. Two traps: hold the NAME across\n`run_passes`, not the `GlobalValue` (see\n[[bugs_llvm_values_dangle_across_the_optimiser]]); and `External` blocks the\nconstant-folding LLVM can do on an `Internal` global, so gate it on the\nmulti-module case rather than applying it to the single-module AOT path. The\nruntime's own store is no use as the binding target as-is: it is\n`thread_local! RefCell\u003cVec\u003cu64\u003e\u003e` and **resizes**, so slot addresses are not\nstable, and it is per-thread while LLVM's inlined globals are not — a\npre-existing LLVM-vs-Cranelift divergence in its own right.\n\nSee [[bugs_benchmark_preset_never_reaches_llvm]],\n[[bugs_llvm_declaration_reuse_collapses_functions]],\n[[bugs_llvm_values_dangle_across_the_optimiser]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_per_function_llvm_promotion` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}